build on Launch Control and the @large pool, de83c802d/271b8f1a2/7bf73f785 split the redesigned panel, and 3a783a411 raised the core floor past the paired release. The one real integration gap was in the shared visual fixture: phase sase-mf.4's _fake_build_launch_model_setting_snapshot stub changed the top-bar launch-default pill from CLAUDE(opus) to CODEX(visual-snapshot-model) in every ACE golden but regenerated only the Models-panel ones. I regenerated exactly the 152 goldens whose sole pixel difference was that pill (verified programmatically per image), deliberately leaving snapshots that also carry unrelated drift so the update could not absorb it -- retired task sase-lo warns about exactly that hazard. just test-visual went 421 failed/269 passed -> 277 failed/413 passed, with zero pill-only diffs remaining (commit 0f63a62ab).\n\nMEMORY FOLLOW-UP: the user authorized the sase/memory/sase_sizes.md migration off @<size>_worker; regenerating it from the updated install template produced content byte-identical to upstream d22622365, so the rebase folded my copy away and the migrated note is on master.\n\nGATES: just check passes every lint gate (fmt, keep-sorted, ruff, mypy, pyscripts, test-waits, changelog, terminology, symvision, toobig) and SASE validation reports no memory drift. just symvision is clean. The escalated full test lane's 85 failures/45 errors are all pre-existing and unattributable to this tree (only .md and .png changed): 33 belong to sase-ml's live-host runtime contamination and 52 to a PatchFilterBar shared-page teardown cluster recorded on active plan sase-m6.6.1.\n\nFOLLOW-UP DISPOSITION: memory doc -> done. sase-core release -> done. sase-mf.3's 'unrelated ruff/symvision noise' -> stale, gates now green. 4 PNG AttributeError tests -> sase-mw and sase-my (my predecessor's duplicates sase-mx and sase-mz closed as superseded). 13 statistics/procs golden diffs + the wider 277 -> recorded as a DISCOVERED ISSUE on in-progress plan sase-m6.6.1, which owns commit 3c3909c31, instead of a new task, since /sase_new_task routes causally linked findings to the active epic. 9 symvision findings -> resolved upstream. 72 test-cost failures -> sase-ml, corroborated with a fresh note."
"LAND: epic sase-fp closes via its landing sub-epic sase-fp.8 (plans:202608/test_selection_landing.md), whose land phase (sase-fp.8.3) performed this close.\n\nVERIFICATION (carried from the landing plan, re-confirmed today): all seven original phases (sase-fp.1-.7) are closed and their deliverables exist -- tests/_test_selection.py, _test_selection_graph.py, _test_selection_contexts.py, _test_selection_health.py, _test_selection_health_plugin.py, _test_selection_fixtures.py, tools/select_tests, tools/selection_health, tools/refresh_contract_manifest, tools/fetch_coverage_contexts, and the test-scoped/test-contexts/refresh-contract-manifest/refresh-contexts-baseline/selection-health Justfile recipes. just check and just check-full share the same eleven-gate list and differ only in the final stage (just test-scoped vs just test), pinned by tests/test_justfile_lint.py. The contract manifest is in sync (test_contract_manifest_matches_marker_selection passes). Integration with post-epic commits was already reviewed and is complete (test_run_pytest_tool.py split, setup-sase composite action change, close-history commits, progress_fingerprint symvision fix, upload-artifact@v4 pin -- no contract-set membership changes, no integration gaps).\n\nTWO DEFECTS FIXED (epic sase-fp.8, commits e7917a268 correlate + 3e8600168 budget):\n\n1. Budget guard (sase-fp.8.1, 3e8600168): the wall-clock ceiling on the nested serial contract-set run was measuring host load, not the set's size (failed real CI at commit 1da5a3e27, recurred 10+ times locally). Replaced with a calibration-probe-normalized child-CPU measurement (resource.getrusage(RUSAGE_CHILDREN) deltas, bracketed by a probe before and after, normalized against a fixed baseline). Verified passing under synthetic 96-spinner-process load where the old wall-clock assertion would have failed (inner wall clock read 42.8s). _BUDGET_SECONDS stays 30.0; current normalized reading is 22.6-23.2s (~7s headroom). Settled sase-fp.2's deferred contract-set membership question: both test_suite_gate_integration.py (+4.8s normalized) and test_markdown_template_packaging.py (+2.0s) stay OUT of the contract set -- every change that could break either already forces the full suite through the broadening rules.\n\n2. False-negative correlation (sase-fp.8.2, e7917a268): find_false_negatives paired ANY non-escalated scoped manifest with ANY full-run failure sharing a HEAD ancestor, so the host-local shared store charged one workspace's flakes to every other workspace's selection (9 false negatives / 32 matches at d66101e8f, none genuine). Full-run and scoped health records now carry workspace identity and change-set identity (schema 1 -> 2); find_false_negatives requires same workspace, HEAD ancestry, and the full run's change set to be a superset of the scoped run's. Pre-schema-2 records are skipped and counted, not silently dropped. The matching rule is stated in both the module docstring and every just selection-health report.\n\nCORRECTED METRIC READING (re-run today, 2026-08-06, after this land phase's own just check-full added another data point):\n  false negatives: 0\n  scoped runs recorded: 12 (10 pre-schema-2, excluded from correlation; 2 schema-2, correlatable)\n  full-lane runs recorded: 25 (22 pre-schema-2, excluded; 3 schema-2, correlatable)\n  32 records total predate schema 2 and are excluded from correlation.\nThe epic's stated exit criterion -- zero false negatives across at least 30 varied changes -- is NOT met: the correlatable sample is only ~2-3 schema-2 records. The reading is honest (0-of-small-sample, not 0-of-clean), and the sample will grow through normal agent usage over the coming weeks; that is what makes the question answerable at all, not a claim the criterion is satisfied today.\n\nFOLLOW-UPS FILED (via /sase_new_task, one at a time, from this land phase sase-fp.8.3):\n  1. sase-ft (medium) -- share the import-graph cache host-locally (sase-fp.1)\n  2. sase-fu (small) -- re-measure the SASE_TEST_SELECTION_DEPTH table before recommending depth 3 (sase-fp.1; depth-3 median measured 9.9% vs the plan's assumed 5.5%)\n  3. sase-fv (medium) -- correlate GitHub Actions failures with local selection manifests (sase-fp.5)\n  4. sase-fw (medium) -- attribute the escalation-rate delta to coverage contexts, revisit the 0.25 max-ratio (sase-fp.6)\n  5. sase-fx (small) -- coverage-contexts baseline availability beyond 14-day retention (sase-fp.6)\n  6. NOT filed as a new task -- the load-sensitive flake family (sase-fp.3/.4/.5/.6/.7) duplicates two existing umbrella tasks; corroborated with +1 evidence instead: sase-e2 (bead-mutation lock timeout, now +25) and sase-ct (ACE TUI stall-watchdog/app-title/notification-gate/prompt-bar/codeblock-highlight nodes, now +12, three of which were new nodes on that bead)\n  7. sase-fy (large) -- break up the 497-module src/sase import cycle (epic plan, root cause of unsound static selection)\n  8. sase-fz (xsmall) -- import Pillow lazily in tests/ace/tui/visual/conftest.py (epic plan, carried from Tier 0)\n  9. NOT filed as a new task -- the three dismissed-bundle scale tests (epic plan, carried from Tier 0) exactly duplicate existing ready task sase-fg; corroborated with a +1 instead\n  10. sase-g0 (medium) -- speed up test_contract_manifest_matches_marker_selection's whole-repo collect-only, newly discovered during sase-fp.8.1 (measured 32.4s vs the plan's ~21s estimate)\n  11. sase-g1 (small) -- full-run failure recorder must drop xdist worker-crash node IDs (e.g. 'gw22'), newly discovered during sase-fp.8.2\n\nVERIFIED: just install + just check-full green on the combined tree (all eleven lint/validation gates plus the full test suite) at commit 3e8600168, run fresh during this land phase."
"LANDED epic sase-pv. All nine phases closed; every phase note re-read and checked\nagainst the source and the epic's nine commits (24ce7e056, 88d2a1582, 98b27e849,\nc5a0dcf4a, 65a34b909, 2b2c5edef, a469015dc, a317a2e35, 281f3c197).\n\nVERIFIED (step 1)\n- The `flag` issue type is gone, not merely unused. `IssueType` is exactly\n  {plan, phase, task}; `FlagRecord`, `BeadFlagWire`, `flag_codec`, `FlagScope`,\n  and the `flag(...)` create grammar have no remaining reference in src/ or\n  tools/. sase-core shipped the matching wire deletion and it is RELEASED, not\n  just committed: v0.29.0 (d80fa83 \"feat(bead)!: delete the flag issue type\",\n  3f4e773 reserved-slug drop, c121e0e task_type_fields on update). The linked\n  sase-core checkout is clean at fa7ced2 with no uncommitted work, closing out\n  sase-pv.4's \"still uncommitted\" caveat.\n- Only `beta` and `sunset` kinds survive and `default` is derived from `kind`.\n  `sase flag new` requires --when-enabled/--when-disabled/--remove-when (each\n  accepting @<path>), offers -k {beta,sunset}, and has no --scope.\n- The seven-field `flag` task type is declared in this project's sase/sase.yml\n  with glyph U+2691, accent #FF875F, agent_creatable false, triage\n  min_plus_ones 0, and the committed sase/task_types.json snapshot agrees.\n- The five live flags are typed task beads: sase-qe, sase-qf, sase-qg, sase-qh,\n  sase-qi. `sase bead list -T flag` shows all five with their preserved sizes\n  and countdowns (88d/88d/89d/89d/90d, all v0.18.0); `sase bead show sase-qe`\n  renders the typed FLAG block plus the rendered body template and its\n  PROVENANCE note naming sase-nw.\n- sase-pv.7's CARRY-OVER is discharged: the five tombstoned flag event streams\n  (sase-nw, sase-nx, sase-om, sase-pa, sase-pk) are gone from the store, so\n  nothing still carries issue_type \"flag\".\n- `just _lint-flags` green; `sase bead doctor` reports nothing flag-related\n  (its remaining warnings are pre-existing and unrelated).\n- The epic's own DISCOVERED ISSUE note (three deterministic failures at\n  a2357e214: the completion-spec drift and the demo_flag(default=...) integrity\n  test) was fixed inside sase-pv.6 and is confirmed dead: tests/feature_flags,\n  tests/completion, tests/task_types, and tests/test_bead/test_flag_fields.py\n  run 287 passed, 1 skipped.\n- Memory and docs are regenerated: sase/memory/sase_flags.md and\n  feature_flags.md describe two kinds and the Off-branch removal rule, and no\n  doc, memory note, or AGENTS.md text still calls a flag an issue type.\n\nINTEGRATED (step 2)\n- Real gap found and fixed. sase-pv.8 added\n  `require_rust_binding(\"bead_needs_drop_flag_type_migration\")` and\n  `..._drop_flag_type_migration_sql\")`, which exist only in sase-core 0.29.0,\n  but pyproject.toml still declared `sase-core-rs>=0.27.18,<0.28.0`. The window\n  excluded every release that has them, so CI's release-core-floor-smoke leg\n  (`tools/check_sase_core_rs_bindings` against the exact declared floor) would\n  have failed and a published install would have crashed on the first bead\n  read. This is precisely the skew that gate was written for. No phase could\n  have caught it: sase-core-rs 0.29.0 was not uploaded to PyPI until\n  2026-08-19T00:50:15Z, after sase-pv.8 closed. Fixed with the sanctioned tool,\n  `tools/ratchet_core_window`, taking the window to `>=0.29.0,<0.30.0` and\n  regenerating uv.lock. `tools/probe_core_floor --advisory` went from\n  \"stale_actionable: missing 11 capability(s)\" to clean; it had named this\n  epic's d80fa83 for two of the eleven. The other nine belonged to the\n  task-type and workspace-occupancy epics and are fixed by the same ratchet.\n- That ratchet exposed a second defect it had to fix to land:\n  tests/test_powerful_variables_landing.py hardcoded the ceiling\n  (`,<0\\.28\\.0$`) in _CORE_FLOOR_RE, so any window move made the guard fail\n  with \"sase-core-rs dependency is missing from pyproject.toml\". That defeats\n  the guard's own documented intent (\"Compare rather than pin ... an\n  exact-string guard would fail every ratchet\"). The regex now accepts any\n  ceiling and still compares the floor.\n- Reviewed every non-epic commit since 24ce7e056 for conflict or duplication.\n  Nothing else needed rework: `sase flag new`'s @<path> support already routes\n  through the shared `sase.cli_file_values.read_at_path_value` added by\n  771454166 rather than duplicating it; 509170484's flake triage bump does not\n  touch the flag type's `triage.min_plus_ones: 0`; and the stale --epic-symbol\n  entries sase-pv.1 and sase-pv.5 had re-keyed to sase-pq and sase-pw are gone,\n  resolved by a3765f857 and 8437cfd9c.\n\nFOLLOW-UPS (step 3) - both phase proposals routed, no new bead created\n- sase-pv.8's flake, test_ace_page_fast_startup_is_structurally_quiet: duplicate\n  of open task sase-oz. Corroborated with `sase bead +1 sase-oz` (now +7),\n  contributing the detail earlier reports lacked - the leftover work is a\n  cancelled `sase-artifacts-project-choices` task, not just \"a worker\".\n- sase-pv.9's flake,\n  test_cross_navigation_and_escape_surface_disabled_workspaces: duplicate of\n  sase-qo, which sase-qd.land root-caused and fixed at 2026-08-19T01:12:58Z\n  (ec048b168's resolve-completion repaint overtaking OptionHighlighted and\n  repainting from the stale bookmark). Not +1'd: the bead is closed with a real\n  fix and my repro tree predates it, so a +1 would only record stale-window\n  evidence. It reproduces 2/3 on master de06c55ca because that fix has not been\n  committed yet; it is sase-qd's to land, not this epic's.\n- Every other PROPOSED FOLLOW-UP across the nine phases was already resolved on\n  master and needed no bead: the glossary/render.py mypy failure (959d205ca),\n  the project_accent/project_accent_map and ledger_path/read_ledger_records\n  symvision unused-publics (a3765f857, 8437cfd9c; `just _lint-symvision` is\n  green), and tests/_suite_gate.py exceeding the toobig limit (bbd3bf212, filed\n  and closed as sase-q7).\n\n`sase bead epic-symbols sase-pv` reports no entries. All whole-repo lint gates\npass on the landed tree: fmt (python, markdown), keep-sorted, ruff, mypy,\nchangelog, toobig, pyscripts, test-waits, patch/stitch terminology, symvision,\nSASE validation, and committed plans.\n\nFOLLOW-UP (step 3, third outcome) - corroborated, no new bead\n- The `lint (feature flags)` gate went red on the landed tree at 2026-08-19T01:45Z\n  with `rule 8: live flag bead 'sase-qq' has no definition (key\n  'plugin_catalog_scoped_latest')`. Not this epic's: sase-qq was created\n  2026-08-19T01:21:12Z by sase-qn.2 (still RUNNING at the time) and its registry\n  definition is on neither this HEAD nor origin/master. It is the known shared-store\n  race already filed as task sase-pp, which had been closed `canceled` on\n  2026-08-18T15:14:02Z precisely because this epic was redesigning flags, then\n  reopened by a +1 on 2026-08-18T22:47:22Z. I corroborated it with `sase bead +1\n  sase-pp` (now +2), contributing what no earlier report could: the first instance\n  observed AFTER this redesign landed, plus proof the redesign left the defect\n  untouched - `_LIVE_FLAG_STATUSES` in src/sase/feature_flags/beads.py is the same\n  five-status set before (88d2a1582~1) and after this epic, and `create_flag_bead`\n  still mints the bead at `open`. So the cancel reason is spent and the bead's own\n  \"create pre-live, promote on the definition commit\" option is still the live\n  candidate. Closing this epic does not resolve sase-pp and must not be read as\n  resolving it.\n- Consequently the lint-gate claim above reads: every whole-repo lint gate passes on\n  this tree except `lint (feature flags)`, whose single finding is that external\n  orphan bead. `tools/check_feature_flags` reports exactly that one line and nothing\n  flag-related to this epic.\n\nFINAL VERIFICATION (2026-08-19, three monitored runs)\n- Lint gates: monitor 0j26dcy3j867 passed every whole-repo lint gate on this tree, then\n  died to a 15m idle timeout inside run_silent-wrapped `test-cost` -- a silent-but-healthy\n  run, not a failure. Re-verified after this round's edit to\n  tests/reproducible_flake_baseline.txt: keep-sorted, toobig, and SASE validation green,\n  and tests/test_selection_health_tool.py plus the four flake-gate modules run 52 passed.\n  `lint (feature flags)` stays red on the single external orphan bead sase-qq only\n  (sase-pp above, now +2); nothing flag-related to this epic.\n- Full suite: monitor 3019d9kftfq1, `just test-cost` --\n  3 failed, 33849 passed, 12 skipped in 750.30s (0:12:30). Every failure is a\n  parallel-lane flake that passes serially on this exact tree, and every one is owned:\n  * test_candidates_fast_path_wall_clock_budget[agent] -- ALREADY baselined under\n    sase-pg, so not new work. Measured 153.5ms against a 150ms budget on gw5 under 16\n    workers; serially the probe is min 101ms / median 114ms over 10 runs and the node\n    passes 18/18 params on 5/5 runs. Confirmed unrelated to this epic: the completion\n    fast path belongs to epic sase-oc and the failing param is `agent`, not `flag`.\n  * test_cross_navigation_and_escape_surface_disabled_workspaces -- sase-qo, below.\n  * test_ace_page_fast_startup_is_structurally_quiet -- ALREADY baselined under sase-oz.\n- Flake baseline gate: `just selection-health --fail-on-new-flake`, check-full's last\n  step, had never produced a result in any earlier run of this landing. It was RED on\n  three reproducible flakes exceeding tests/reproducible_flake_baseline.txt and is now\n  GREEN: \"no new reproducible flakes (12 current, 25 allowed)\". Resolved as follows.\n\nFOLLOW-UPS (step 3, final round) - three corroborations plus one baseline edit, no new bead\n- tests/ace/tui/modals/test_snippet_name_modal.py::test_new_trigger_returns_empty_starting_body:\n  semantic duplicate of snoozed task sase-og, not a new bead. sase-og describes a fixed\n  `pause(0.25)` read of an