"ROOT CAUSE FOUND FOR A GAP THIS EPIC DOES NOT COVER (new epic plan proposed: monitor_supervisor_survival). On 2026-08-13 12:53:44 agent zl.w0--code ran 'sase monitor start --command \"just test-visual\" --timeout 20m --next <continuation>'. No monitor ever ran; the lane went silent 17m33s and the --next continuation (the whole remaining artifacts_split_modes.md plan) was discarded.\n\nEVIDENCE (monitor 18tx7ty20h8w, artifacts .../ace-run/202608/13/20260813125344): agent_meta.json has NO monitor_output_path (supervise.py:137, the supervisor's first action after _read_meta) and NO monitor_pgid (supervise.py:192); live_reply.md is exactly 73 bytes -- only sase-ku.5's reconciler line; supervisor.log exists and is 0 bytes. Since the supervisor now runs with stdout=supervisor.log/stderr=STDOUT, a traceback would have been captured (verified live: 'python -m sase monitor _supervise --artifacts-dir <missing>' prints a full traceback and total startup is ~0.83s). So the supervisor was killed by a signal DURING its own ~0.8s startup -- before signal.signal() at supervise.py:133, before reading meta, before spawning the command.\n\nWHAT KILLS IT: maybe_handoff_monitor_from_agent -> kill_agent_runner_group() SIGTERMs the runner group ~0.1s after start_monitor returns, i.e. ~10% into the supervisor's startup. killpg alone does NOT explain it -- start_new_session=True (start.py:214) protects it, and I reproduced the exact topology (runner pgroup / isolated-pgid child / start_new_session grandchild / killpg(runner_pgid, SIGTERM)): the grandchild SURVIVES. What does explain it is a perfect correlation with the starter's runtime, because setsid escapes a killpg but not a PPID walk, and at kill time the supervisor is still a descendant of the runtime's Bash tool:\n  codex starters:  smoke-fail--0 (exit 3, ran), z2--code 'just check-full' (ran 30s, exit 1)  -> SURVIVED\n  claude starters: sase-kp.land--code 'just check-full', sase-kv.5.w1--code 'just test-visual', zl.w0--code 'just test-visual' -> ALL DIED, 0 output, exit_code null\n  host-started (starter_agent null): zl.f1, zm, z6.f2, sase-kp.land.w1 -> all fine\nPer the Uniform Agent Runtimes rule the fix is runtime-agnostic (double-fork so the supervisor is reparented to PID 1 before start_monitor returns, plus signal dispositions set in the supervisor's first statements), not a claude special case.\n\nWHY NOTHING NOTICED (four gaps, all outside sase-ku.1-.9): (1) start_monitor returns monitor_state='running' without ever confirming the supervisor is alive, and its caller's next act is to kill itself; (2) handlers installed ~0.8s late; (3) run_stale_running_cleanup (axe/hook_jobs.py:375-399) wraps _reconcile_dead_monitor_supervisors in 'except Exception' and lets cleanup_stale_running_entries release the ace-monitor claim anyway -- workspace #10 was handed to sase-ku.9--plan at 12:54:35, 51s into a 20-minute test run, and workspace prep stashed zl.w0--code's 23-file/756-line implementation into stash@{0} of sase_10 (recoverable, but only by luck of the stash); (4) that harvest then killed the follow-up: launch_followup_agent (followup.py:116-133) passes retry_transfer_from_pid=<dead supervisor pid> and failed with 'workspace #10 with pid 3333672 was not found', discarding the --next payload into a done.json string.\n\nCREDIT WHERE DUE: sase-ku.5's reconciler is the only reason this surfaced at all (it moved the phantom to failed at 13:11:17), and sase-ku.3's durable identity is what let it decide the supervisor was dead. This epic hardened the supervisor once it is running; the new plan covers dead-on-arrival. Expected file overlap: monitor/start.py, supervise.py, followup.py, settlement.py, transaction.py, ace/scheduler/stale_running_cleanup.py, axe/hook_jobs.py -- disjoint from sase-ku.10, which commits no source."
